# Week 6 – Problem Sheet (Deep Reinforcement Learning)

## DQN vs PPO
- DQN is a value-based, off-policy method that learns a Q-function.
- PPO is a policy-based, on-policy method that directly optimizes the policy.
- DQN is more sample efficient due to experience replay.
- PPO provides more stable learning and works well in continuous action spaces.

## Policy-based vs Value-based Methods
- Value-based methods are suitable for discrete action spaces.
- Policy-based methods naturally handle continuous actions.
- Policy gradients allow stochastic policies and smoother exploration.

## Effect of Replay Buffer Size
- Small replay buffers lead to correlated samples and unstable learning.
- Larger buffers improve stability but may slow adaptation to new data.

## Why PPO Clipping Helps
- PPO clipping restricts large policy updates.
- This prevents performance collapse during training.
- In experiments, smaller clip values led to more conservative updates,
  while the standard clip value (0.2) allowed better peak performance.